==============================================================================
                               v101-00
==============================================================================
                               GENERAL
==============================================================================
Cloonix is an AGPLv3 software that creates networks of kvm virtual machines
and crun containers.  It is a network virtualisation hypervisor: the topology
is built, driven and observed from a web browser, on an interactive canvas.

The network that links the guests together is an openvswitch instance running
inside a private linux net namespace, so the network configuration of your
host is never modified by cloonix.

Everything cloonix needs is embedded in the delivery: no library of the host
is used and nothing else has to be installed.  The binaries are compiled
inside a debian trixie virtual machine.

Two packagings are provided, a tar.gz and a tar.xz (tar xf needs xz):

  cloonix-bundle.tar.gz   system-wide install, root needed at install time
  cloonix-toy.tar.xz      user-land only, root never needed

and the toy specialised for one open source project, containers only:

  cloonix-frr-toy.tar.xz  cloonix-bird-toy.tar.xz
  cloonix-suricata-toy.tar.xz  cloonix-bind-toy.tar.xz

2 brandtypes of guests run in cloonix:

  kvm    real virtualisation run by qemu-kvm, root file-system is a .qcow2
  cnt    container run by crun, root file-system is a directory or a
         .tar.gz that cloonix extracts once

The graphical interface is a web page served by cloonix itself.  There is no
gtk client any more, no X11 forwarding, and no wireshark or spice binary to
install on the host: the packet capture viewer (webshark) and the guest
desktop (noVNC) both live inside the browser page.

The canvas is served on one https port, https://<ip>:4444, reachable from
anywhere.  The certificate is self-signed, the browser warns once.

The web page asks for a user and a password.  They are not secret, they are
shipped in the configuration, and they can be changed there:

  https://127.0.0.1:4444      user  nemo      password  nemoclown

  the file, bundle      /usr/libexec/cloonix/cloonfs/etc/cloonix.cfg
  the file, toy         <toy directory>/rootfs/cloonix/cloonfs/etc/cloonix.cfg

The user is the name of the net, the password its cloonix_passwd in that
file, which the client's cloonix_config.py links to: the name of the net
followed by clown.  For the net nemo: user nemo,
password nemoclown.

See clownix.net for the documentation:
https://clownix.net/build_user/user.html


==============================================================================
                          BUNDLE INSTALLATION
==============================================================================
The bundle installation is based on cloonix-bundle.tar.gz, it copies the
binaries to /usr/libexec/cloonix/cloonfs, puts the cloonix command and the
cloonix_*.py files it imports in /usr/bin, and uses /var/lib/cloonix as work
zone.

Root privileges are necessary for the installation, and since some cloonix
actions deal with namespaces and network interfaces, one binary,
cloonix-suid-power, gets the suid root permission.

1) Download and install:
------------------------

wget https://clownix.net/downloads/cloonix-bundle.tar.gz
tar xvf cloonix-bundle.tar.gz
cd cloonix-bundle
sudo ./install_cloonfs

To uninstall:

sudo rm -f /usr/bin/cloonix*
sudo rm -rf /usr/libexec/cloonix
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/cloonix
sudo rm -rf /usr/share/cloonix

The install erases every /usr/bin/cloonix* before it copies: cloonix is the
one name to type, and a name used by an older release must not stay on the
PATH. Beside it go the cloonix_*.py files it imports, one per verb that is
large enough to be worth keeping out of the entrance -- files, the demo
player, the drawing of a topology, the words a tab offers.


2) Download the guest root file-systems:
----------------------------------------

mkdir -p /var/lib/cloonix/bulk
cd /var/lib/cloonix/bulk

qcow2 (virtual machine for qemu-kvm):
wget https://clownix.net/downloads/kvm_bulk/trixie.qcow2.gz
gunzip trixie.qcow2.gz

cnt (container image for crun, used as it is: the first add cnt on it
extracts it once into trixie_targz, read only, for every net and user):
wget https://clownix.net/downloads/cnt_bulk/trixie.tar.gz

A container that keeps its changes (--persistent) needs the directory form,
extracted under another name:
mkdir mytrixie && tar xpf trixie.tar.gz -C mytrixie


3) Start a network and open the canvas:
---------------------------------------

cloonix start nemo

Then point a browser at:

    https://127.0.0.1:4444

The port is the "cloonix_web" value of the net in cloonix.cfg, it is 4444 for
nemo, 4454 for mito, 4464 for fido.


4) Play a demo:
---------------

cloonix cli nemo demo                        lists what is installed
cloonix cli nemo demo security/wg.py      plays one

The demos are in /usr/share/cloonix/demos: a subject area, then one python
file per demo, and the windows, freebsd and cisco demos at the top.  The name
can be shortened to the programme (wg or wg.py).


==============================================================================
                            TOY INSTALLATION
==============================================================================
The cloonix-toy needs no privileges, neither at install nor at run time.
It is a user land application, it only needs a linux distribution to run.
Cloonix runs inside a rootless crun container, the whole file tree stays in
the directory you extracted.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Download:
------------
wget https://clownix.net/downloads/cloonix-toy.tar.xz

2) Extract the directory:
-------------------------
tar xvf cloonix-toy.tar.xz

3) Call the setup.sh script to adapt the tool to its env:
---------------------------------------------------------
cd cloonix-toy
./setup.sh

The setup.sh takes an optional cloonix net name, "nemo" is the default:

./setup.sh mito

It creates a command script named after the chosen net: ./nemocmd, and it
prints a small report on the host devices it will need.

4) Start the server:
--------------------
./nemocmd start

5) Open the canvas in a browser:
--------------------------------
https://127.0.0.1:4444

6) Play a demo:
---------------
In the canvas, the Demo button of the header lists the demos and plays
them; Delete all erases every object without stopping the server.

7) Enter the container that hosts cloonix:
------------------------------------------
./nemocmd shell

8) Kill all:
------------
./nemocmd stop


NOTE:
=====
For cloonix to be able to run the qcow2 files, the devices /dev/kvm,
/dev/vhost-net and /dev/net/tun must have read/write access for the user
launching the demonstration:

sudo setfacl -m u:${USER}:rw /dev/kvm
sudo setfacl -m u:${USER}:rw /dev/vhost-net
sudo setfacl -m u:${USER}:rw /dev/net/tun

For cloonix to be able to use the wifi emulation, the module mac80211_hwsim
must be inserted in the kernel:

sudo modprobe mac80211_hwsim radios=0

On ubuntu the unprivileged user namespaces may have to be unlocked:

    kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=1
    kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0

On fedora and centos, selinux may have to be set to disabled in
/etc/sysconfig/selinux.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------


==============================================================================
                          NOTES FOR THE CANVAS GUI
==============================================================================

https://127.0.0.1:4444 gives the canvas gui.  A right-click on the empty
canvas gives the creation menu: kvm, cnt, lan, tap, nat, a2b, c2c, and phy
with one line per physical interface of the server.

To cable an item to a lan: double-click the lan (it turns purple, it is
selected) then single-click the small numbered circle on the border of the
machine.  That small circle is an ethernet interface.  An interface is born
without capture capability and its circle is cyan; "Sniff on" in its menu
adds the capability and the circle turns green, "Sniff off" removes it.

A double-click on a machine opens a root shell inside the browser page.
A double-click on a green interface opens webshark, the embedded packet
capture viewer.
A right-click on a kvm gives its menu: Desktop (noVNC), Console, Save,
Reboot, Delete, Info; on a cnt: remote console, info, delete.
A right-click on an interface gives PlotTraffic, a live throughput plot, and
Sniff on / Sniff off.
A right-click on an a2b item gives Tune a2b: loss, delay and rate per
direction.

The header bar has the "Demo" button, which opens the Demo panel: Play
picks a demo from the packages of the course, Commands prints the commands
that rebuild the current topology.  The "PcapRec" button records all the
capturable interfaces at once into .pcap files, and the "Delete all" button
erases every object of the net.

==============================================================================


==============================================================================
                              CLIENT COMMANDS
==============================================================================

There is a single command, cloonix, and it takes a verb:

cloonix list                            every net of the config, and the versions
cloonix start <net>                     starts the server of a cloonix network
cloonix stop <net>                      destroys every object and the server
cloonix cli <net> <verb> ...            add del plug unplug cnf dump sniff save ...
cloonix cli <net> ssh <name> ["cmd"]    root shell inside a guest, no ip needed
cloonix cli <net> scp <src> <dst>       file transfer to and from a guest
cloonix cli <net> files <n> [<n>]       two panes, host and guest or two guests
cloonix cli <net> demo [name]           list the demos, or play one
cloonix cli <net> trace probe <name>    where the pipe into a guest breaks

The <net> is one of the names declared in cloonix.cfg: nemo, mito, fido ...

A verb may be written short where the letters typed fit one verb only, three
of them at least: "cloonix cli nemo tra qem" is trace qemu, "cloonix cli nemo
dum top" is dump topo. Names are typed whole.

Every command begins with cloonix; there is no shell of its own to sit in.

Nothing is installed in any shell and no directory of the machine is touched
for it, but tab is there for whoever wants it, in a bash or a zsh:

eval "$(cloonix --completion)"

Then tab after "cloonix " offers the verbs, and tab further along offers the
nets, the cli verbs and the guests of a running net. The words come from
cloonix_completion.py of the delivery, so what is offered is what the client
accepts.

A line short of a word asks for the rest at a prompt of its own, runs what
it ends up with, and is gone. What it built is not handed to the history of
the shell: up at the shell gives back what was typed there, the bare
"cloonix" that opened the prompt.

cloonix writes nothing on the machine it runs on and reaches into nothing of
it -- no history file, no config file, nothing under the home directory, and
nothing added to the shell that launched it. A command ends on its own
report and on nothing else.

cloonix is python3 using nothing but the standard library -- the cloonix
file, the cloonix_*.py files beside it and cloonix_config.py -- so the same
files run on windows and on macos as well as on linux, with nothing to
install on the client machine. It reaches a cloonix running elsewhere over
the tcp port of the net.

Away from linux: windows has no termios, so raw keystrokes are taken through
its console api instead, and an interactive shell and the full screen
programs inside it work there as they do here -- in a real console, the api
refusing a pipe and the mintty of git bash; pulling a directory that contains
symlinks needs windows developer mode; and cloonix start, which starts a
server, is local to the linux machine that hosts cloonix. Commands, cli and
file transfer are unaffected.

win_client/ installs that client on windows 10 and windows 11, for the case
where a cloonix command is wanted rather than a file to name a python with:

./win_client/make_win_zip.sh

writes /tmp/cloonix-client-win-<v>.zip.

Carry the zip to the windows machine, extract it, run install.bat. It takes
the newest python there, 3.7 or later, copies the client and the demos under
%LOCALAPPDATA%, puts cloonix on the PATH and registers an uninstall. No
administrator, nothing compiled, nothing downloaded, no package installed.

Example of a two machine network, the server started in a terminal of its
own and the other commands typed in a second one:

cloonix start nemo
cloonix cli nemo add kvm kvm1 ram=1000 cpu=2 eth=1 trixie.qcow2
cloonix cli nemo add kvm kvm2 ram=1000 cpu=2 eth=1 trixie.qcow2
cloonix cli nemo plug kvm1 eth0 lan1
cloonix cli nemo plug kvm2 eth0 lan1
cloonix cli nemo ssh kvm1 "ip addr add dev eth0 1.1.1.1/24"
cloonix cli nemo ssh kvm1 "ip link set dev eth0 up"
cloonix cli nemo ssh kvm2 "ip addr add dev eth0 1.1.1.2/24"
cloonix cli nemo ssh kvm2 "ip link set dev eth0 up"
cloonix cli nemo ssh kvm1 "ping -c 5 1.1.1.2"

